{
  "_comment": [
    "Design series, and the colloquial names people search by.",
    "",
    "'Series' is the industry's word, not one invented here: PCGS organizes its",
    "price guide, population report and CoinFacts by series, so using the same",
    "term is what lets a coin be looked up against a published value.",
    "",
    "label is the formal name; aliases are what people actually say. That split",
    "is load-bearing rather than decorative. Measured against this collection's",
    "own descriptions: 'Mercury' appears 104 times and 'Winged Liberty Head'",
    "zero; 'Buffalo' 156 and 'Indian Head Nickel' zero; but 'Walking Liberty'",
    "130 against 'Walker' 2. Neither the formal names nor the nicknames alone",
    "would find everything.",
    "",
    "Paper money is cataloged by Friedberg number, and",
    "its classes are already in note_type, so no parallel vocabulary of note",
    "classes belongs here. What does: the handful of established collector",
    "names for specific issues -- Funnyback, Barr Note, Hawaii, North Africa --",
    "which name a denomination and series exactly, as a coin series names a",
    "design. Facts and sources are in",
    "docs/specs/series-classification-design.md.",
    "",
    "denomination and series_year_range are what app.series_classify assigns",
    "from: a design issued at that denomination in that year. A design with no",
    "range rows is matched on its own span. Ranges exist where one span is",
    "wrong (Morgan: 1878-1904, 1921, 2021 on) or where a note design pairs",
    "different denominations with different series (Hawaii: $1 1935A; $5, $10",
    "and $20 1934 and 1934A). letters narrows a note's series letter: null is",
    "any, '*' is none. The bullion designs and the multi-face gold designs",
    "carry no denomination and are matched by name only: a Silver Eagle's $1",
    "face would make every dollar coin since 1986 a boundary case.",
    "",
    "needs_evidence marks a design that shares its denomination and years with",
    "a far commoner one: a Hawaii note with every 1934 $5, a commemorative half",
    "with the Kennedy half, a gold dollar with the Morgan. It is assigned only on",
    "evidence -- text naming it or, for a note, a matching seal_color -- so an",
    "item that says nothing is taken for the common design.",
    "",
    "Dates are the years the design was struck. year_end is null for a series",
    "still in production.",
    "",
    "The early cent designs (Flowing Hair to Braided Hair) are the Large Cent's",
    "own types and need evidence, so a cent that says nothing stays a Large",
    "Cent. Half dimes are five-cent pieces and share that denomination with",
    "the nickels; early gold spans several faces and is matched by name, as",
    "Liberty Head Gold is. The large-size note designs are the collector",
    "names in general use for one denomination and series of one note class.",
    "Sources for every design are in the specification named above.",
    "",
    "The Trade Dollar needs evidence: it shares 1878-1885 with the Morgan and",
    "1873 with the Seated Liberty dollar, and was struck for commerce abroad,",
    "so a dollar of those years that says nothing is the common design."
  ],

